export const HUMAN_SURFACE_MD = "# Human Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem\n\n**Date:** 2026-11-19\n**For:** James (Founder, Project Lead, 456iP)\n**To be sent to:** Jonathan Schmidt — Creative Intelligence Principal, L2Launch (Santo Domingo, DR) — for his input\n**Program:** HUMAN ECOSYSTEM DESIGN — the human-facing design ecosystem of UZE\n**Status of the system:** House skill RATIFIED by James, 2026-11-19 11:15 ET. Rollout order approved; Stages 1–4 proceeding with notice to James.\n\n---\n\n## What this document is\n\nThis document refines the core design plan created earlier today (2026-11-19) — the HUMAN ECOSYSTEM DESIGN anchor report, the ratified house skill (`uze-design-system`), the canonical token file, and the rollout plan — into a single statement of the **Human Design Surface**: the design system that governs every surface a human being sees, reads, or touches anywhere in the UZE ecosystem.\n\nIt exists for two readers:\n\n- **James**, who ratified the system this morning and holds the tap on every change to it.\n- **Jonathan Schmidt**, who is being asked for design evaluation input. Jonathan's input does not land as edits to pages. It returns as **proposed amendments** to the canonical token file, which James ratifies or declines; a ratified amendment then propagates to every anchored surface at once, because every surface resolves its values from the one source. Section 6 states exactly what input is being asked for.\n\nA companion document, **Agentic Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem**, covers the counterpart system — the AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN, named by James on 2026-11-19 as this system's pair. The two surfaces are segregated deliberately: this document governs what humans see; the companion governs how agents present, identify, and account for themselves. Neither document borrows the other's rules.\n\nThis is a document for review and input. Nothing in it authorizes building, sending, or spending. The build state it reports in Section 5 was authorized separately, by James's taps of 2026-11-19 11:15 ET.\n\nThe system's sources of record, which this document refines and does not replace:\n\n- Canonical tokens: `~/workspace/design-anchor/tokens.json` (v1.0.0, RATIFIED)\n- The rules in words: the house skill, `~/workspace/custom-skills/uze-design-system/SKILL.md` (RATIFIED)\n- Design sample of record: the approved v5 UZE email (`apps/dev-library/lib/mailer.mjs` in the monorepo)\n- The verification gate: `~/workspace/tools/design-gate/` (v0.2), with its proof record\n- The anchor kit: `~/workspace/design-anchor/` (DESIGN.md template, agent-instructions snippet, car-wash workflow template, tenant-override spec, rollout plan)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The surface defined\n\n### 1.1 What the Human Design Surface is\n\nThe Human Design Surface is the complete, executable design system for every human-facing surface in the UZE ecosystem: web pages, the UZE Dev Library, emails, generated documents that carry the brand, and the rendered output of any repo in the estate — including surfaces that do not exist yet and surfaces deployed under a client's own brand.\n\nIt is a system, not a style guide. A style guide is a document a person may or may not open. This system is built so that the standard is **executable**: the values are data (one canonical file), the rules are written once (one house skill), every repository carries an anchor that points at both, and a verification gate — the car wash — sits in the deploy path of every anchored repo, so a page that violates the system does not deploy. The sensitivity is resident in the machinery, not dependent on anyone remembering it.\n\nThe system was created on James's direction of 2026-11-19 (~00:06 ET): the design sensitivity must be a global sensitivity — anchored in every new repo and all existing repos, resident in any white-label or multi-tenant engagement, and run as a pre-deployment car wash before any page deploys — so that simplistic design errors stop costing the founder hours of catching them by eye. The immediate cause was concrete: on 2026-11-18, two errors reached James's phone that a written system plus a verification step would have caught first — a wordmark that wrapped to two lines at phone width, and red and green status colors sitting in a page whose approved system contains no red or green anywhere.\n\n### 1.2 Who it serves\n\n- **Humans on every UZE surface.** Readers, development partners, talent, clients, and the public, on uzemarkets.com, dev.uzemarkets.com, the AEInfo surfaces, Fundio, the shared Command & Control Console, and every surface that follows. UZEmarkets launches January 2, 2027 and is a prospective project; Learn2Launch is the present phase. This system governs the design of both, and does not change what either is.\n- **Enterprise and larger-SME white-label clients ($15–35M+ class).** Engagements of that size require extreme UI/UX sensitivity as a condition of the work. Under this system a tenant deployment inherits the full standard — structure, verification, and all — and customizes only token values (Section 3.4). The design sensitivity stays resident inside the client's deployment because it is enforced by the gate in their deploy path, not by a document handed over at kickoff.\n- **The people who build and steer.** James, who ratifies; Jonathan Schmidt and his Fort Lauderdale motion-graphics collaborator, who advise; and every human or agent who opens a repo and meets the anchor before touching a branded surface.\n\n### 1.3 The pairing with the Agentic Design Surface\n\nJames named two counterpart design systems on 2026-11-19:\n\n- **HUMAN ECOSYSTEM DESIGN** — this system. Human-facing surfaces: what a person sees, reads, and judges.\n- **AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN** — the counterpart. Agent-facing surfaces and the infrastructure agents act through, covered by the companion document, *Agentic Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem*.\n\nThe pairing matters for one practical reason: a change is always filed under exactly one of the two. A palette value, a wordmark treatment, a page layout — human surface, this document, amendment to `tokens.json`. An agent identity, audit, or verification surface — agentic surface, the companion document. Nothing is allowed to drift between the two by being governed by neither.\n\n---\n\n## 2. How it works — the five-part mechanism\n\nThe mechanism is one chain of five parts. Each part exists because the part before it is not sufficient alone.\n\n### 2.1 One canonical source of values\n\nEvery design value in the ecosystem resolves from a single file: `~/workspace/design-anchor/tokens.json` — the eight-token palette, the type scale, component geometry, the wordmark rules, and the standing rule that no other hues exist anywhere, status feedback included (Section 3 states the values in full).\n\nBeside it, `tokens.css` carries the same values as CSS custom properties. It is generated from the JSON, and the two were verified mechanically at creation: identical color and size value sets. The house skill carries the same system as rules in words — why each rule exists, and the mandatory pre-ship check.\n\nNothing else is a source. No repo, page, email, or agent keeps its own divergent copy of the values. When a surface and the design sample of record (the approved v5 email) disagree, the email's treatment governs until James rules otherwise.\n\n### 2.2 A per-repo anchor\n\nEach repository carries the anchor — four artifacts, defined in the rollout plan:\n\n1. **`DESIGN.md`** at the repo root: one read that gives any human or agent opening the repo the non-negotiables and the pointer to the canonical tokens.\n2. **An agent-instructions block** in the repo's agent-instructions file (`AGENTS.md` / `.github/muse-instructions.md`), so the encounter with the system is unavoidable before branded work starts.\n3. **The car-wash workflow and a `design-wash.json`** declaring the repo's wash targets, wired so the repo's deploy path depends on the wash (Section 2.3).\n4. **A token link, not a token copy.** The anchor points at the canonical `tokens.json` / `tokens.css`; surfaces resolve values from there (or from a build step that pulls them), so a versioned amendment propagates instead of stranding dozens of divergent copies.\n\nNew repos are born anchored: the template set is part of repo creation, so the estate stops growing unanchored repos.\n\n### 2.3 The car wash in the deploy path\n\nThe design gate (`~/workspace/tools/design-gate/`, currently v0.2) is the wash: one command —\n\n```bash\nnode gate.mjs <page-file-or-local-url> [--out <dir>] [--wordmark <css-selector>]\n```\n\n— that runs four checks and exits non-zero on any failure (exit 0 pass · 1 fail, with the failures named · 2 gate could not run):\n\n1. **Palette lint.** Every hex color in the page must be one of the eight house tokens, with occurrence counts named for any offender. For URL targets, the page's same-origin stylesheets are fetched and linted with the HTML — a served app's palette lives in its CSS. Inline script contents are excluded from the corpus (framework payloads are not authored design); authored colors render through stylesheets, style elements, and style attributes, all of which stay in the corpus. A fully transparent hex (alpha `00`) renders as no color and is counted, never a violation; a semi-transparent color lints on its RGB, so a black scrim must be an ink scrim.\n2. **Renders.** Full-page screenshots at 420px (phone) and 1280px (desktop), saved beside the report as evidence.\n3. **Wordmark one-line check at 420px**, measured on the live render, in two parts: the wordmark element must carry `white-space: nowrap` (the guarantee — rendered fit in one engine is font-metric luck, and the 2026-11-18 wrap reached a phone whose render differed from desktop Chromium), and it must occupy exactly one line (rendered height ÷ computed line-height ≤ 1.25) with no horizontal overflow. The rendered-lines measure is live independently: a forced break fails even with `nowrap` present. On component-built sites the wordmark is not the header `h1`; `--wordmark <css-selector>` names the element that carries it (uze-web: `--wordmark \".wordmark\"`). The rule checked is identical.\n4. **Axe accessibility floor.** axe-core runs on the 420px render; violations are printed by rule id with impact and node count, and recorded in the report. Findings are currently **report-only** — a deliberate posture, so pre-existing findings do not train everyone to ignore the gate. Promoting violations of impact critical/serious to hard failures is the documented next step for the gate.\n\nIn CI, the wash is a required job on the repo's deploy path: the deploy job `needs:` the wash, so **a failed wash fails the deploy — no wash, no deploy.** The wash's evidence (report plus both screenshots) is uploaded as a run artifact on every run.\n\nThe gate was proven before it was wired anywhere, under the rule that every check must be able to fail: the current Dev Library page passes (exit 0); a mutant with the exact red that shipped on 2026-11-18 reintroduced fails, naming the palette violation; a mutant with the wordmark's `nowrap` removed fails, naming the one-line violation — even though that mutant still happens to render on one line in headless Chromium, which is precisely why the check enforces the rule and not just the render. The full record is in the gate's `PROOF-2026-11-19.md`.\n\nThe wash is mechanical *and* visual. The gate's screenshots are looked at by the builder before ship — because the three-second test (Section 3.5) is judgment, and judgment is in the checklist, not the linter.\n\n### 2.4 White-label: token value overrides only\n\nA tenant engagement supplies one override file (`tenant.tokens.json`, kept with the tenant's deployment configuration) containing only the keys they override, in the same schema as the canonical tokens. At build/deploy time the deployment's **effective tokens** are computed — canonical tokens with the override values merged over them, key by key — the surface is built from the effective tokens, and **the gate runs against the effective tokens**, plus every structural check unchanged.\n\nA tenant **may** override values only:\n\n- **Palette values** — a full re-palette is allowed; it is still exactly eight tokens, one accent, and no hue outside the tenant's own declared set. The no-conventional-status-hues rule binds tenants too.\n- **Wordmark text and logo** — the companion word or product name beside the mark, and a tenant logo asset — subject to the structural wordmark rules, including one line at every width.\n- **Type families** — family substitution only; the scale relationships do not move. Licensed fonts are the tenant's responsibility.\n- **Imagery** — real hosted assets; never emoji as icons, never placeholder boxes in a shipped page.\n\nA tenant may **never** override:\n\n- The one-line wordmark rule (a tenant name that doesn't fit gets smaller, never wrapped).\n- Type-scale relationships (headline > card title > section head > body > secondary > meta, at the canonical ratios).\n- Component geometry (pill buttons at radius 999px, card radius and hairline borders, band treatments, hairline-only dividers, outline-only tags).\n- The feedback rule (status renders in the text token with the accent marker; no tenant gets red/green status colors — the rule is structural, not a UZE palette preference).\n- The accessibility floor (a tenant palette whose accent fails contrast on its ground fails the wash; the fix is a tenant value change, never a waived check).\n- The car wash itself (no tenant deployment path may skip, weaken, or self-certify the gate; same gate, same exit codes, same evidence per deploy).\n- The three-second test (no version numbers, runtimes, or production notes on any public tenant surface either).\n\nA tenant value change is an edit to their override file plus a passed wash — no approval from James needed for tenant-internal values. A request that needs a structural change is not a tenant override at all: it is a design-system amendment (Section 2.5), ratified by James, and then available to every deployment. Tenants are never forked onto private structures. Full spec: `~/workspace/design-anchor/tenant-override.spec.md`.\n\n### 2.5 Advisory input as amendments, never as edits\n\nWhen James, Jonathan Schmidt, or his Fort Lauderdale motion-graphics collaborator steers the design, the input lands as a **versioned amendment** to `tokens.json` — version bump plus a dated note in the house skill — ratified by James. It then propagates to every anchored repo at once, because they all resolve from the one source.\n\nThe change rule is stated in the canonical file itself: changes are proposed by SoSem or by the design advisory channel (Jonathan Schmidt and his Fort Lauderdale motion-graphics collaborator) and ratified by James. **A page-level edit is never a design-system change.** No one-off page edits, no drift between surfaces, and every change carries a version and a date.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The non-negotiables\n\nThese are the rules the anchor states, the gate enforces where enforcement is mechanical, and the pre-ship checklist carries where it is judgment. They are transcribed from the system James approved — the v5 email — and ratified with the house skill on 2026-11-19. Nothing in this section is a proposal.\n\n### 3.1 Palette only — eight tokens, no other hues\n\n| Token | Value | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ink | `#1a1a1a` | Text, headings, bands, pill buttons |\n| Cream | `#f5f2ee` | Page background, code-block fill |\n| Tangerine | `#ff6b00` | The single accent: \"UZE\" in the wordmark, links, markers, tags |\n| Creamsicle | `#FDEEE2` | Soft-band fill (ink text on it) |\n| Hairline | `#ddd5c9` | Borders, dividers, card edges |\n| Muted | `#6b655e` | Secondary / meta text |\n| Band-muted | `#b8b0a4` | Eyebrow text reversed out of ink bands |\n| White | `#ffffff` | Card surface; text reversed out of ink |\n\n\"Creamsicle\" is James's term for the lighter tint of the brand tangerine — the pale peach accent of the system.\n\n**No other hues. Ever. Status feedback included.** Never red, green, blue, amber, or any \"conventional\" status color, on any surface — pages, emails, documents, generated surfaces. Convention is not a source; this palette is. Opacity/tint derivatives of the eight tokens are the only permitted variation, and a new tint is a system change: it is recorded as an amendment ratified by James, never introduced inside a page. (This is the exact question currently open on uze-web — see Section 4.2.)\n\n### 3.2 The wordmark never wraps\n\n- **\"UZE\" is always tangerine `#ff6b00`.** The word that follows is white reversed out of ink (on a band) or ink (on a light ground). Never tangerine-on-tangerine, never muted.\n- Forms of record: `UZEmarkets` · `UZE Dev Library` · `UZE` + a letter-spaced eyebrow in band-muted.\n- **One line at every width, phone included.** `white-space: nowrap` on the wordmark element; at ≤620px the size steps down (of record: 29px → 23px) — reduce the size, never accept the wrap. A wrapped wordmark is a build failure, on the same level as a failed test.\n- Footer form: `© 2026 UZEmarkets` centered in an ink band — \"UZE\" tangerine, linking to https://uzemarkets.com, plain wordmark look, no underline; \"markets\" white.\n\n### 3.3 Components and layout\n\n- **Buttons are black pills:** ink background, white text, `border-radius: 999px`, sans 700. Primary padding 14px 34px (email) / 11px 22px (page). Secondary: white pill, ink text, ink border. No square buttons, no colored buttons.\n- **Cards:** white surface, 1px hairline border, radius 14px, padding 28px 34px (24px 20px at ≤620px).\n- **Dividers are hairlines:** 1px `#ddd5c9`, full width — never heavy rules, never colored rules.\n- **Ink bands** carry reversed-out content: white display text, tangerine wordmark accent, band-muted eyebrows; radius 14px, padding 20px 34px.\n- **Creamsicle soft bands** carry centered ink content: `#FDEEE2` fill, radius 12px, padding 26px 24px, serif headline 21px.\n- **Tags** are tangerine outline pills — 1px tangerine border, tangerine text, radius 999px, 12px/700 — outline, never filled.\n- **Links** are tangerine, weight 700; underlined in body copy, no underline in the wordmark/footer treatment.\n- **Facts lists** use the tangerine \"—\" marker at weight 800.\n- **Page frame:** max-width 880px centered, cream ground. Generous whitespace is part of the system (the Maple-modeled language James approved) — spacing is not compressed to fit more in.\n- **Imagery:** real hosted assets; never emoji as icons or decoration; never placeholder boxes in a shipped page.\n\n### 3.4 Type\n\n- **Display: Georgia serif** (`Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif`) — headlines, card titles, FAQ summaries, banner headlines. Serif is for display only; body copy set in serif throughout is an out-of-system error.\n- **Body: system sans** (`-apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif`).\n- Scale of record:\n\n| Element | Spec |\n|---|---|\n| Page headline (h1) | Serif 29px / 1.2 / 700 (23px at ≤620px) |\n| Card title (h3) | Serif 21px / 1.25 / 700 |\n| Section head (h4) / FAQ summary | Serif 16–17px / 700 |\n| Body text | Sans 16px / 1.65 |\n| Secondary text | Sans 14px / 1.6 |\n| Meta / muted lines | Sans 13px (tags 12px) |\n| Code numerals | Sans 34px / 800, letter-spacing 8px, centered on cream |\n\n### 3.5 Feedback and states\n\n- Error, success, and notice text renders in **ink**. An error additionally takes the tangerine \"—\" marker (the email's facts marker) at weight 700. Success is plain ink.\n- Feedback sits directly under the control it concerns, and says what happened and what to do next, in plain words.\n- Disabled and loading states use muted text and reduced emphasis within the palette — no new colors.\n- Anti-enumeration silence, where a flow requires it (for example sign-in codes), is a behavior rule of that flow, not a styling exception; the visible message still follows this section.\n\n### 3.6 The pre-ship check and the three-second test\n\nNo page ships without the house skill's §6 check, run in order — a failure at any step stops the ship:\n\n1. Render the final page in headless Chromium at 420px and 1280px; screenshot both, full page.\n2. **Look at both screenshots** — the builder's own eyes on the actual render, not the code. Completion is an observable result in the running system.\n3. Wordmark check: one line at 420px, tangerine UZE, correct companion color.\n4. Palette check: every color value in the shipped markup/styles against the tokens — status colors included.\n5. **Three-second test (James's):** the first phone screen carries **title, subject, hook** — and nothing else competes with them. No version numbers, word counts, runtimes, or production notes anywhere public.\n6. Component pass: pills are pills, dividers are hairlines, bands reverse out correctly, creamsicle bands carry ink text, cards match §3.3.\n7. Accessibility floor: axe-core (or Lighthouse) — no contrast or label violations ship. Automation catches roughly half of WCAG issues; it is a floor, not a certificate. The standing references behind the system are the Nielsen Norman Group usability heuristics and WCAG 2.2 AA.\n8. Regression guard: where the app has a test suite, add the assertions that would have caught the failures of record (palette-token presence plus off-palette absence; wordmark nowrap), so the next change cannot silently reintroduce them.\n\nThe delivery report states the check's result — screenshots, palette result, test tally. A delivery report that makes James ask \"have we tested?\" is a failed report.\n\nSteps 1, 3, 4, and 7 are mechanized by the car wash (Section 2.3). Steps 2, 5, 6, and 8 are the builder's — judgment, deliberately kept human (Addendum, Pillar 7).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Where it stands — rollout state, 2026-11-19\n\nStated by category, because the categories differ and the difference matters: **proven** (wired, washed, deployed, evidence on file) · **wired and held** (the wash stands in the deploy path and is currently stopping a deploy) · **anchored and failing** (anchor in place, pages fail the wash, failures named) · **proceeding** (approved, in the order filed, not yet wired).\n\n### 4.1 Proven — Stage 1, the Dev Library (monorepo)\n\n- James ratified the house skill and approved the rollout order on 2026-11-19 at 11:15 ET.\n- The monorepo (DEVBUILDSTACK/UZE) is anchored: `DESIGN.md` + `AGENTS.md` at root, the design gate vendored under `tools/design-gate`, `design-wash.json` naming the Dev Library page, and a `design-car-wash` job inside `deploy-dev-library.yml` with the deploy job depending on it.\n- **First fully-anchored deploy proven:** commit `4827fdc`; CI run `44564494393` — design-car-wash SUCCESS, then deploy SUCCESS. Local vendored-gate run: PASS (palette 8/8 tokens, wordmark 1.00 line at 420px, axe 0 violations). Live check after deploy: dev.uzemarkets.com 200, /api/catalog 401 (gate intact). Evidence: the CI `design-wash-evidence` artifact and `~/workspace/your_files/design-wash-stage1-evidence/` (report plus both screenshots).\n- The Dev Library page's two pre-existing axe findings (`select-name`, `heading-order`) were fixed the same day; the page runs at zero axe violations.\n- The gate was re-vendored at **v0.2** the same day (commit `d70d7b4`) — `--wordmark` selector, stylesheet linting for URL targets, alpha-00 hexes counted not failed — and CI re-proven on the new version.\n\n### 4.2 Wired and held — uze-web (monorepo, Stage 1 second wire)\n\n- uze-web (uzemarkets.com, Next.js, server-rendered) is wired: a `design-car-wash-web` job in `deploy.yml` builds the app as CI builds it, serves it locally, and washes the routes `/`, `/mcp-utility`, `/knowledge-base` with the wordmark measured on the `.wordmark` element. The deploy job depends on the wash.\n- At wiring, the clear violations were fixed: a pure-black hover and scrim moved to ink, and a debug red (`#d64545`) moved to ink — the ratified rules name those exactly (palette only; no status hues). The wordmark passes on all washed routes (nowrap, one line).\n- **The deploy is currently held by the wash** on four hexes, identical on all three routes: **`#c24e00`** (the site-wide link shade), **`#e05f00`** (a button hover shade), **`#fff4ec`** (an underwrite banner tint), **`#ff9e45`** (the end of an ombre gradient). These are a working tint/shade practice that the eight-token set does not cover. They await **James's ruling**: ratify them as named derivative tokens by amendment to `tokens.json`, or flatten them to the eight. Until he rules, the wash holds uze-web deploys — the gate working as ratified, stated plainly rather than routed around.\n- uze-web also carries report-only axe findings of its own (printed on every wash run; they do not block under the gate's current report-only posture).\n\n### 4.3 Anchored and failing — Stage 2, AEInfo\n\n- The AEInfo repo (DEVBUILDSTACK/uze-aeinfo-prediction-markets) is anchored: commit `722c7c0` — `DESIGN.md` + `AGENTS.md`, vendored gate, `design-wash.json` naming all 11 game-day pages, and a standalone `design-car-wash.yml`. The gate was re-vendored at v0.2 the same day.\n- **Its pages fail the wash, by record.** The placeholder-brand mock pages (\"AEInfo-Prediction UZEmarkets\") were built before the system existed. The gate's first runs name the drift exactly: off-palette near-ink shades (`#141210`, `#1e1b18`, `#2a251f`, `#332e29`, `#a89f96`), a green (`#3ecf6e`), a red (`#ff5a5a`), and no `h1` wordmark. That failure list is the remediation list for the AEInfo go-live build (soccer first, under the filed go-live scope, on its own tap) — the wash was wired now so the standard is already standing when that build starts. The state is recorded in the repo's own `DESIGN.md`; nothing about it is hidden or averaged away.\n\n### 4.4 Proceeding — Stages 3 and 4\n\nUnder the approved order, with notice to James as each lands:\n\n- **Stage 3** — Fundio (user front end, full anchor) · super-admin-panel (admin console, full anchor; load-bearing because the shared Command & Control Console must stay consistent across all three front ends) · assurative-v2 (anchor; wash targets = any branded pages/docs it serves) · monolithic-backend (DESIGN.md + snippet; the anchor governs its emails, documents, and rendered output) · LEAD_GEN (DESIGN.md + snippet; wash targets = its lead-facing pages/emails as identified in the wiring pass). Each wiring is four artifacts plus a wash run on the repo's current pages, reported with evidence; a page that fails its first wash is reported to James as a finding — fixing content is separate work under his direction, never silently folded into a wiring.\n- **Stage 4** — the full sweep of the ~41-repo DEVBUILDSTACK estate, scan-driven rather than hand-listed: every repo scanned for branded-surface signals; surface repos get the full anchor ordered by surface size and liveness; repos with no surface get DESIGN.md + snippet only; the sweep reports a table to James (repo → surface found → anchor state → first-wash result). Nothing is claimed anchored that a scan and a wash run have not shown.\n\n### 4.5 Documented next step for the gate\n\nPromoting axe violations of impact critical/serious from report-only to hard failures in `gate.mjs`. The floor exists and is printed on every run today; the promotion is the gate's next versioned change, and it will be reported the same way the v0.2 changes were.\n\n---\n\n## 5. What input is being asked of Jonathan\n\nJonathan — your lane in this system is **design evaluation advisory input**. You are not being asked to supervise the standard, and you are not being asked to edit any page. The ask is narrower and stronger: steer the system itself, through the amendment channel, where one ratified change improves every surface at once. Your Fort Lauderdale motion-graphics collaborator's input travels the same channel, as part of yours.\n\nConcretely, input is being asked on:\n\n1. **The palette in use.** Eight tokens, one accent, no other hues — including no conventional status colors, anywhere. Where does this hold up under real production work, and where does it strain? If a value is missing, name it as a proposed token: its value, its role, and the surface that needs it.\n2. **The wordmark treatment.** Tangerine \"UZE\", companion reversed out of ink or in ink, one line at every width, size stepping down instead of wrapping. Does the treatment carry at motion/video sizes and contexts your work touches, or does a form need to be added to the forms of record?\n3. **The component language.** Black pills, hairline dividers, ink bands, creamsicle soft bands, outline-only tags, Georgia serif for display only, the 880px frame and its whitespace. What reads as dated, missing, or wrong for the surfaces this system has to carry into the January 2, 2027 launch and beyond?\n4. **Motion and video.** The system as ratified governs static surfaces and email. Motion-graphics and video work under Creative Intelligence will need the system's values and rules expressed in motion — timing, transitions, how the wordmark and bands behave when they move. What should the first motion amendment contain?\n5. **The wash itself.** The gate checks palette, wordmark, renders, and the accessibility floor mechanically; the three-second test and the looked-at render stay human. From a production seat: what is the first check you would add, and what would you never automate?\n\nHow input returns: as proposed amendments — a short note per item naming the token or rule, the proposed change, and the reason — to James (through SoSem). James ratifies or declines each. A ratified amendment is versioned in `tokens.json` with a dated note in the house skill, and propagates to every anchored repo at once. Nothing you propose changes any page directly; that is what keeps forty surfaces from drifting into forty opinions.\n\nOne boundary, stated so it does not have to be discovered: UZEmarkets itself — what it is and how it rolls out — is context for this document, not part of the design ask. The design ask is the system above.\n\n---\n\n## ADDENDUM — Pillars of the Human Design Surface\n\nThe mechanism in Section 2 rests on seven pillars. Each is stated as a principle, then grounded in what the system actually does today. A pillar is load-bearing in the plain sense: remove it, and a named failure from the record returns.\n\n### Pillar 1 — One canonical source of values\n\n**Principle.** Every design value in the ecosystem exists in exactly one place, and every surface resolves its values from that place.\n\n**In the system.** `~/workspace/design-anchor/tokens.json` (v1.0.0, RATIFIED) holds the palette, type scale, component geometry, wordmark rules, and the no-other-hues rule. `tokens.css` is generated from it and was verified mechanically identical in value sets. The house skill carries the same system as rules in words. Repos hold links, never copies (Pillar 5's overrides and Section 2.2's anchors both resolve back to this file). The file's own meta states the rule: every repo anchor, the design gate, and every tenant deployment resolve their values from this file or from a versioned amendment to it — no repo keeps its own divergent copy.\n\n**What it prevents.** Drift by accumulation: forty repos each remembering the palette slightly differently, until \"the brand\" is forty approximations. With one source, an amendment is one edit with a version and a date, and it propagates everywhere at once.\n\n### Pillar 2 — Palette only: no hue exists outside the tokens\n\n**Principle.** The eight tokens are not the preferred colors. They are the only colors. A hue that is not in `tokens.json` does not exist on any UZE surface — in layout, in components, in charts, and in status feedback above all.\n\n**In the system.** Ink `#1a1a1a`, cream `#f5f2ee`, tangerine `#ff6b00`, creamsicle `#FDEEE2`, hairline `#ddd5c9`, muted `#6b655e`, band-muted `#b8b0a4`, white `#ffffff`. Errors, successes, and notices render in ink; an error adds the tangerine \"—\" marker. Red, green, blue, and amber are forbidden by name in the canonical file's rule and in the house skill. The palette lint enforces the pillar mechanically on every wash: any other hex is a named failure with its occurrence count. Opacity/tint derivatives of the tokens are the only permitted variation, and a genuinely new tint is an amendment (Pillar 6), never a page-level decision.\n\n**What it prevents.** The 2026-11-18 failure exactly: red `#b02a37` and green `#2e7d32` status colors in a page whose approved system contains neither — introduced because convention (\"errors are red\") silently outranked the system. Under this pillar, convention is not a source. The pillar is also why the four uze-web tint/shade hexes in Section 4.2 are an open ruling rather than a quiet exception: the system does not do quiet exceptions.\n\n### Pillar 3 — The wordmark never wraps\n\n**Principle.** The wordmark is one line at every width, on every surface, without exception. If it does not fit, it gets smaller. It never breaks.\n\n**In the system.** \"UZE\" is always tangerine; the companion word is white reversed out of ink or ink on a light ground — never tangerine-on-tangerine, never muted. The element carries `white-space: nowrap`; at ≤620px the size steps down (of record, 29px → 23px). A wrapped wordmark is classified as a build failure, on the same level as a failed test. The gate checks the pillar two ways at 420px: the computed `nowrap` must be present (the guarantee), and the rendered result must be exactly one line with no horizontal overflow (the confirmation) — each measure able to fail on its own, as the gate's mutant proofs show. On component-built sites, `--wordmark` names the element that carries the mark; the rule checked is identical. The footer form is fixed: `© 2026 UZEmarkets`, \"UZE\" tangerine linking to https://uzemarkets.com, no underline.\n\n**What it prevents.** The other 2026-11-18 failure: \"UZE Dev Library\" reaching the founder's phone with \"Library\" alone on line two — although it plainly fits on one line. The pillar's sharp edge is the `nowrap` requirement: a wordmark that merely happens to fit in one rendering engine is luck, and the system does not ship luck.\n\n### Pillar 4 — The car wash: no page deploys unwashed\n\n**Principle.** Verification is not a step a builder remembers to do. It is a gate in the deploy path that a page cannot pass without satisfying — palette lint, renders at phone and desktop widths, the wordmark check, and the accessibility floor, with the evidence kept.\n\n**In the system.** The design gate (`~/workspace/tools/design-gate/`, v0.2) runs as a required CI job; the deploy job `needs:` it, so a failed wash fails the deploy. Exit codes are part of the contract: 0 pass, 1 fail with named failures, 2 gate could not run — and \"could not run\" is not a pass. Every run retains its evidence (report plus 420px and 1280px screenshots) as a deploy artifact. The gate was proven able to fail before it was trusted to pass anything: injected off-palette color, removed `nowrap`, and a forced break each fail with the violation named. The first fully-anchored deploy (Section 4.1) is this pillar operating end to end — wash SUCCESS, then deploy SUCCESS, in that order, in CI, on the record. Axe findings are report-only today by deliberate posture; promoting critical/serious findings to hard failures is the documented next step, and when it lands it lands as a versioned gate change, not a quiet tightening.\n\n**What it prevents.** The whole class of failure the founder named on 2026-11-18: simplistic errors he had to catch manually, by eye, on his phone, across repeated iterations — because nothing mechanical stood between a page and production. The pillar moves the catching to the deploy path, where it happens every time, the same way, whether or not anyone is watching.\n\n### Pillar 5 — White-label by token override only: the sensitivity stays resident\n\n**Principle.** A tenant changes values. A tenant never changes structure, and never touches the wash. The design sensitivity therefore survives white-labeling intact — it is resident in the deployment, not loaned to it.\n\n**In the system.** The tenant supplies one file, `tenant.tokens.json`, holding only the keys they override. Effective tokens are computed by merging those values over the canonical set; the surface builds from the effective tokens; the gate runs against the effective tokens with every structural check unchanged. Overridable: palette values (still exactly eight tokens, one accent, no hue outside the tenant's declared set, no conventional status hues), wordmark text and logo, type families, imagery. Never overridable: the one-line wordmark rule, type-scale relationships, component geometry, the feedback rule, the accessibility floor, the three-second test — and the car wash itself, which no tenant path may skip, weaken, or self-certify. A tenant palette that fails contrast fails the tenant's deploy; the fix is a value change, never a waived check. A request for structural change leaves the tenant channel entirely and becomes a system amendment under Pillar 6.\n\n**What it prevents.** The usual white-label dilution: the tenant's team edits pages directly, the standard degrades into a PDF nobody opens, and the enterprise client's product quietly becomes sloppy in ways the client can feel but not name. Here the only tenant-writable surface is a value file the gate reads. The tenant can make the product unmistakably theirs; they cannot make it off-system or unwashed — and neither can we, on their behalf or ours.\n\n### Pillar 6 — Advisory input as ratified amendments, never page edits\n\n**Principle.** Design steering — from James, from Jonathan Schmidt, from his Fort Lauderdale motion-graphics collaborator — enters the system as versioned, ratified amendments to the canonical source. It never enters as an edit to a page.\n\n**In the system.** The canonical file's change rule names the channel: amendments are proposed by SoSem or by the design advisory channel and ratified by James, landing as a version bump to `tokens.json` plus a dated note in the house skill. Because every anchor resolves from the one source (Pillar 1), a ratified amendment propagates to the whole estate at once — no surface is left carrying the old value, and no surface carries a private improvement the rest of the ecosystem never received. Tenant structural requests route into this same channel (Pillar 5). The house skill's own history is the pattern: drafted 2026-11-19, ratified by James's tap at 11:15 ET the same day, standing practice from that tap — a version, a date, a named ratifier.\n\n**What it prevents.** Two opposite failures: drift (good input applied to one page and lost to the rest) and capture (a change entering the system without the founder's ratification because it arrived as a small edit somewhere). Under this pillar every design decision has exactly one door, and the door has a name and a date on it.\n\n### Pillar 7 — Judgment stays human: the three-second test and the looked-at render\n\n**Principle.** Automation is the floor; the look is the standard. The checks that are judgment — hierarchy, emphasis, whether the first screen earns its three seconds — are assigned to human eyes by rule, and are never delegated to the linter or averaged into a score.\n\n**In the system.** The house skill's pre-ship check requires the builder to look at both screenshots — the actual render, not the code — and applies James's three-second test: the first phone screen carries title, subject, hook, and nothing else competes with them; no version numbers, word counts, runtimes, or production notes anywhere public. The car wash produces the renders on every run precisely so the look has something to land on; the gate report records the mechanical results, and the delivery report carries the wash result — screenshots, palette result, test tally — so the judgment is exercised on evidence, not on recollection. The supporting practice is named in the skill: the Nielsen Norman Group heuristics for flows, WCAG 2.2 AA as the accessibility floor, and the rule that completion is an observable result in the running system, not a claim about the code.\n\n**What it prevents.** The false comfort of a green checklist on a page no one actually looked at — the failure mode in which every automated check passes and the page is still wrong in the way only a person glancing at a phone would notice in three seconds. The founder's eye caught the 2026-11-18 errors because his eye was the only check standing. This pillar keeps the eye — his, the builder's, the advisors' — and removes its loneliness: it is now the last check in a line, not the only one.\n\n---\n\n## Sources and record\n\n- Anchor report: `~/workspace/research_notes/design-capability-2026-11-19/HUMAN-ECOSYSTEM-DESIGN-ANCHOR-REPORT.md`\n- House skill (RATIFIED 2026-11-19 11:15 ET): `~/workspace/custom-skills/uze-design-system/SKILL.md`\n- Canonical tokens (v1.0.0, RATIFIED): `~/workspace/design-anchor/tokens.json`; generated CSS beside it as `tokens.css`\n- Rollout plan and wiring log: `~/workspace/design-anchor/ROLLOUT.md`\n- Tenant override spec: `~/workspace/design-anchor/tenant-override.spec.md`\n- Per-repo anchor template: `~/workspace/design-anchor/DESIGN.md.template`; agent-instructions snippet and car-wash workflow template in the same kit\n- Design gate (v0.2): `~/workspace/tools/design-gate/` — `README.md` and `PROOF-2026-11-19.md`\n- Stage 1 evidence: CI run `44564494393` (DEVBUILDSTACK/UZE) and `~/workspace/your_files/design-wash-stage1-evidence/`\n\n*End of document. Prepared 2026-11-19 for James; to be sent to Jonathan Schmidt for input under Section 5.*\n";
export const AGENTIC_SURFACE_MD = "# Agentic Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem\n\n**Date:** 2026-11-19\n**For:** James (founder, Project Lead at 456iP) — and, on his send, Jonathan Schmidt (L2Launch Creative Intelligence Principal), for his input.\n**Status:** A design document for review and input. It refines the agentic design material established 2026-11-18/19. **Nothing in this document authorizes building, sending, spending, or trading.** Every build, listing, and money movement named here stands behind James's tap, exactly as it does today.\n\nThis document is one of a pair. Its counterpart, the **Human Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem**, covers the design system for human actors. This one covers the design system for non-human actors. The two are named together — the **AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN** and the **HUMAN ECOSYSTEM DESIGN** — and they are designed together, because in this ecosystem the same work is seen by a human on one surface and done by an agent on the other.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Preamble\n\nOn 2026-11-19 James named this system: the **AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN**. In the same direction he named its **Fundamental Pillars** — the three pillars of the Coinbase Ventures \"Beyond the bot\" agentic-trading stack mapping, explored the same morning:\n\n1. **Agentic trading harnesses and terminals**\n2. **Market reasoning models and data assets — a real edge**\n3. **Identity, audit trails, and performance-verification infrastructure for non-human financial decision-makers**\n\nThe naming sits on a principle James set the night before (2026-11-18): **always develop Agents-first**, with efficient and effective agent-to-agent (A2A) commerce and data-science best practices observed. James weighed the principle against the prediction that the overwhelming majority of financial transactions will happen between agents within ten years, and adopted it as a standing rule — through the full chain, from scope to build, and out into how agent-facing surfaces are tagged, found, and engaged.\n\nThis document states the surface plainly: what it is, how it works, where it honestly stands, the pillars it rests on (Addendum A), and the two financial domains it touches — kept separate, because they are different domains (Addendum B). It is written for James's review and for Jonathan's input; §7 states exactly what input is being asked.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The surface defined\n\n### 2.1 What the Agentic Design Surface is\n\nThe Agentic Design Surface is the design system for **non-human actors**: software agents that **discover, negotiate, transact, and settle** across the UZE ecosystem on behalf of a principal — a person, a desk, or an enterprise — who sets the mandate and remains accountable for it.\n\nA human using UZEmarkets reads pages, weighs judgments, and taps approvals. An agent using UZEmarkets does none of that. It connects to a machine surface, presents a wallet-bound identity, states what it is authorized to do, calls tools, pays per call, and leaves a complete record. The design problem is therefore different in kind from the human surface:\n\n- A human surface must be **legible** — a person must understand it at a glance.\n- An agent surface must be **bounded** — an agent must be unable to exceed its mandate, and every boundary must be enforced by the system, not requested of the agent.\n- A human surface earns trust by **presentation**.\n- An agent surface earns trust by **record** — identity, gating, and an audit trail that a principal can inspect after the fact and rely on.\n\nEvery rule in this document follows from those four verbs — discover, negotiate, transact, settle — and from one constraint that governs all of them: **the agent acts; the principal answers for it; the system proves what happened.**\n\n### 2.2 The pairing with the Human Design Surface\n\nThe two surfaces are one system seen from two sides, joined by the **dual-surface contract**:\n\n- **The console is a view over the API.** Anything a human sees in a console exists because the underlying machine surface produced it. There is no human-only back door and no agent-only shadow system.\n- **Autonomous-first for money and execution; human-first for judgment.** Agents execute inside mandates without waiting for a person. Decisions that are judgment — approving an assessment's validity, accepting an engagement, ruling on a claim — are reserved to humans by construction, not by policy. In the Talent system, two human gates stand before any assessment runs, and an agent identity can satisfy neither gate. That is the contract working as designed.\n- **One chronicle under both.** A human's approval and an agent's call are events in the same record, so a principal reviewing a console sees the whole history of a matter — human and machine actions interleaved, in order.\n\nThe HUMAN ECOSYSTEM DESIGN (ratified by James 2026-11-19) governs how all of this looks and reads to people: the tokens, the wordmark, the car wash every page passes before it deploys. The AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN governs how all of it behaves for machines. Neither is complete without the other; a surface that is beautiful to a human and unbounded for an agent is a liability, and a surface that is rigorous for an agent and illegible to its principal is unaccountable.\n\n### 2.3 The frame: optimizing enterprise innovation\n\nThis surface exists for one purpose, stated as James directed it: **optimizing enterprise innovation.**\n\nEnterprises do not innovate by adding dashboards. They innovate when work that used to wait on a person — sourcing, matching, underwriting, allocating, settling — can proceed continuously, inside limits they set, with proof they can audit. The agentic surface is what makes that possible inside the UZE ecosystem:\n\n- **Discoverable** — an enterprise's agents can find the ecosystem's desks and tools through the same directories and tagging the agents themselves use (§3.1, §3.8).\n- **Gated** — no agent acts outside a pre-authorized mandate; limits are set by the principal before the first call, and enforced before any call executes (§3.3).\n- **Auditable** — every call is a chronicle event; the record is the product's memory and the principal's evidence (§3.4).\n- **Settle-able** — value moves on a stated rail law, the same law for a human-initiated payment and an agent-initiated one (§3.7).\n\nAn enterprise that can send its agents into a market with those four properties can experiment at machine speed without surrendering control. That is the innovation this surface is designed to optimize — and it is why the treasury domain in Addendum B, Part 2, is the ultimate focus: an enterprise's own balances are the first place this discipline pays for itself.\n\n---\n\n## 3. How it works\n\n### 3.1 MCP-first surfaces — the agent door\n\nThe ecosystem's agent door is the **Model Context Protocol (MCP)**. Every capability offered to agents is offered as an MCP tool: named, described in one canonical language, tier-gated, and metered.\n\n**UZE Instant MCP** is the platform's single endpoint (mcp.uzemarkets.com). It comes in two tiers:\n\n- **UZE Instance** — the generic tier: branded compute plus settlement, the nine base tools, no vertical access.\n- **UZE Markets Instance** — the premium tier: the base tools plus the vertical pathways into the ecosystem's desks and markets.\n\nThe catalog of record is **18 tools — 9 base + 9 vertical**. The base tools cover the instance lifecycle (provision, credentials, status, terminate), balance, and payment (`pay_via_x402`); the escrow pair (`create_escrow`, `release_milestone`) belongs to the settlement phase. The vertical tools open the desks — including the three Talent tools (`submit_assessment`, `score_assessment`, `publish_assessment_outcome`), which are live.\n\n**Distribution is directories.** Agents find tools where agents look: the LLM and MCP directories. The filed strategy is two-tier — a conversion tier first (the Anthropic MCP registry, then Smithery, then Cursor/Windsurf), a reach tier after (OpenAI, Perplexity, DeepSeek, Venice, Kimi, Hostinger, and the open MCP directories). One descriptive language serves the listings, the site's MCP section, and the Knowledge Base identically, so no surface invents its own wording. **No listing is claimed anywhere until it is verified live** — the standing rule is that listings claim only what is green.\n\n### 3.2 Wallet-bound agent identity\n\nAn agent's identity is its wallet. Sign-in to the Instant MCP is a wallet signature: the signature is the credential, the wallet is the account, and every authorization hangs off that binding. There is no separate agent username to issue, leak, or revoke — revoking the wallet's authorization revokes the agent.\n\nThis is the root of the trust model. A principal can answer, at any time: *which wallet acted, under whose pre-authorization, within what limits, and what exactly it did* — because identity, limits, and record all hang off the same binding.\n\n### 3.3 Three-layer gating — enforcement before execution\n\nNo call executes until it has passed three layers, in order:\n\n1. **User pre-authorization.** The principal sets the fence in advance: per-call and per-day ceilings, and optionally a tool allowlist. This layer is the mandate, stated by the human or enterprise that owns the wallet.\n2. **MCP enforcement.** The endpoint checks every call against the fence *before* execution — not after, not on a sampling basis. A call outside the fence is refused at the boundary, and the refusal itself is recorded.\n3. **Vertical gates.** Behind individual tools stand the vertical's own gates. Some are mechanical (tier, balance). Some are human by construction: the Talent vertical's two gates — an engaging employer's confirmation of intent, and an approver of record's confirmation of validity — cannot be satisfied by any agent identity, at any tier, ever.\n\nRevocation cascades at the same boundary: when a principal revokes, sessions die, later calls refuse, and an in-flight call completes — a clean edge, demonstrated in the Phase 1 proofs.\n\n### 3.4 The chronicle — every call an event\n\nEvery call, grant, and refusal is written to an **append-only chronicle**. The chronicle is not a log file kept for debugging; it is a designed component of the surface — the shared record under both the human console and the agent endpoint (§2.2), and the raw material of every audit, report, and performance claim the ecosystem will ever make about agent activity.\n\nIts current limit is stated plainly in §4 and Addendum A: the chronicle is a record, not yet a *proven tamper-proof* record. Closing that gap is Pillar 3 work.\n\n### 3.5 x402 metering — payment as a protocol act\n\nAgent activity is metered with **x402**: payment is part of the call, not a monthly invoice reconstructed afterward. An agent pays per call from its wallet, inside its pre-authorized ceilings, and the payment is itself a chronicle event. Metering does three kinds of work at once: it prices the surface, it rate-limits by economics rather than by courtesy, and it makes every unit of agent activity carry its own cost record — the data-science substrate for understanding what agents actually do in the ecosystem, which the agents-first principle requires be observed with best practices, not guessed at.\n\n### 3.6 Settlement — one rail law\n\nValue settled through the agentic surface follows the same **rail law** as everything else in the ecosystem (James, 2026-11-18). There is no separate agent rail:\n\n1. **USDC on Solana is the default for everything.**\n2. Cascade, only if the prior rail is unavailable: **USDC on Base** →\n3. **USDG on Robinhood Chain** →\n4. **Tempo/Stripe CASH** — only if none of the first three is available, and never a default.\n\nTwo carve-outs are part of the law:\n\n- **CASH is excluded from SigLayer and TradeLayer entirely** — those surfaces settle on the first three rails only.\n- **Inside the World.xyz/AEInfo vertical, CASH is the required default** — the one vertical where the cascade starts at the fourth rail, because that vertical's own settlement asset requires it.\n\nTwo role definitions keep the law intelligible. **UZE counts and credits usefulness** — measurement and attribution of the usefulness an offering delivered. UZE is the brand and the count/credit unit: **not a token, not money, never a payment method.** **UZD is a reward only** — it accrues from counted, credited activity as an incentive and attribution instrument. **No UZD ledger exists yet**, and nothing in this document claims one.\n\n### 3.7 Discovery through tagging — SEO and GEO for agents\n\nThe agents-first principle extends past the endpoint: **agent engagement through app tagging for SEO and GEO**, and savvy, strategic development of social-feed-bound content with optimal execution practices across social and messaging channels (James, 2026-11-18, 22:43 ET extension).\n\nIn practice: the ecosystem's pages and listings are tagged so that search engines, generative engines, and agent frameworks can find, parse, and correctly describe what each surface does — the dual-surface discipline the HIADR Content Desk already embodies (every post exists as human analysis and as machine-readable structure with transact endpoints). An agent that cannot discover a desk cannot use it; discovery is therefore a designed layer of this surface, not a marketing afterthought. The directory strategy (§3.1) and the tagging discipline are the same strategy at two depths: be listed where agents look, and be legible to whatever comes looking.\n\n### 3.8 Two MCP families — never blended\n\nThe ecosystem's MCPs fall into two families, and the families are kept apart by rule (James):\n\n- **Product MCPs** — **UZE Underwrite** (the Autonomous Underwriting desk), **UZE Assign** (the DealFi matching desk), **UZE Auction** (sealed-bid secondary auctions). These face the ecosystem's desks and markets.\n- **Trading MCPs** — **TradeLayer** and **SigLayer**. These face trading and settlement.\n\nThe **Brokerage MCP** is the TradeLayer door, built on the Robinhood model: a user connects their own LLM to their brokerage accounts through a dedicated, separately funded account, with every order reviewed before it executes. Crypto held in Robinhood custody is Robinhood's, and is labelled as theirs — never presented as UZE's crypto solution. The build order is fixed: **Robinhood MCP first, then TradeLayer launches with it, then SigLayer as full production once TradeLayer works.**\n\nThe matching desks come online in their own order — **DealFi (DFI) first, then Fundio (FVC)** — under the line that names them: *Autonomous Underwriting, Talent & DealFi matching desks — UZEmarkets superIntelligence.*\n\n---\n\n## 4. Where it stands\n\nHonest state, as of 2026-11-19. Three categories: what is built and proven, what is built but not yet live, and what is not built.\n\n**Built and proven**\n\n- **UZE Instant MCP, Phase 1** — built and verified 2026-11-18: wallet-bound sign-in, three-layer gating, the chronicle, x402 payment tooling, and the revoke cascade, each demonstrated in the Phase 1 proofs (including the three gating demonstrations the merged Scope 1 + Scope 3 program required).\n- **7 of 9 base tools live.** The escrow pair — `create_escrow`, `release_milestone` — is dark until the settlement phase, by design.\n- **The Talent tools are live** through Scope 2: assessments can be submitted, scored, and published (redacted, pseudonymous) through the endpoint, with the two human gates enforced in the vertical, outside the endpoint's power to satisfy.\n- **The MCP utility surface** — the Robinhood-modelled brokerage door — exists as a spec of record and a live directory mock-up on the site, on demo data. The descriptive language for every MCP (one language, three surfaces) is filed.\n\n**Built but not live**\n\n- **Provisioning runs on a labelled local mock.** The Instant MCP's provisioning leg demonstrates the full flow against a mock that says it is a mock. No real instances are provisioned, and no spin-up time is claimed anywhere — the \"<30 seconds\" figure is withheld from every surface until it is measured.\n- **The public endpoint is not open.** mcp.uzemarkets.com opens with the January 2, 2027 launch. The live-endpoint proof — the Phase 1 system exercised against a reference client over the real endpoint — waits on two things: **NEAR AI Cloud credits** (both keys authenticate against a zero balance; live inference returns a payment error) and a **deployment decision**.\n- **No directory listing is claimed live anywhere.** The listings work is tracked, the language is written, and nothing is represented as listed until it is verified.\n\n**Not built**\n\n- No live brokerage connection; no real-money trading of any kind.\n- No trading-hardened harness skills (Addendum B, Part 1).\n- No market reasoning model, no simulated-trading environment, no verified strategy marketplace (Addendum A, Pillar 2).\n- No tamper-proof chronicle, no verified performance tracking, no agent reputation system (Addendum A, Pillar 3).\n- No treasury product (Addendum B, Part 2) — that part of this document is a design surface and a direction, not a shipped service.\n\n**Proposals on record (unratified).** A 2026-11-07 direction proposes NEAR as the intent/identity/agent-control plane, AssurativeV2 as the one core, USDC on Solana as settlement of record, and Altitude as treasury home, with confidential computation routed between NEAR AI Cloud and Arcium where it does not suffice — the \"confidential by default\" direction. These are **proposals, not ratified architecture**, and are labelled as such wherever this document relies on them (notably Addendum B, Part 2).\n\n**Money gates in force.** Money stays gated by James through December 2026. The single standing exception-in-waiting is the **$100 XSigLayer envelope** (granted 2026-11-09, expires January 2, 2027), which is **pending activation** — no keys are held and nothing has traded under it. Under the SigLayer mandate as it stands, **no agent trades, ever**; the reporting discipline described in Addendum B is observation and audit, not execution.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Addendum A — Pillars of the Agentic Design Surface\n\n### A.1 Fundamental Pillar 1 — Agentic trading harnesses and terminals\n\n*The decision loop: an agent holds a persistent objective, does its own research, sizes positions, executes, and monitors risk, while the human moves from operator to principal — setting the mandate, not placing the trades.*\n\n**What UZE has.** The harness-shaped pieces exist: the MCP utility (a connect-your-own-LLM terminal surface with a dedicated, separately funded account and review-before-action), the Instant MCP endpoint with its gating and chronicle, and the matching desks as decision-loop infrastructure. The form factor is right: intent in, fenced execution out, everything on the record.\n\n**What is missing.** The harness is not trading-hardened — no dynamic position sizing, no take-profit/stop-loss discipline, no backtest-before-live-money mode (the exact gap the Coinbase Ventures mapping names; the remedy is scoped in Addendum B, Part 1). The brokerage door runs on demo data; there is no live brokerage, no retention or volume evidence, and no demonstrated conversion of a user's stated intent into a working portfolio. Intent-as-a-Service is the direction this pillar points in — per the value-accrual mapping, owning the decision loop is where value concentrates — but it is a direction, not a product.\n\n### A.2 Fundamental Pillar 2 — Market reasoning models and data assets — a real edge\n\n*Intelligence is the layer that decides whether the harness's actions are worth taking. The durable asset is proprietary data and the reasoning built on it — the \"context loop\" in which use improves the model that serves the next use.*\n\n**What UZE has.** Two genuine data assets. The **QuantGPT data plane** serves the Delphi information-markets program. **UZE Underwrite's same-micro-region benchmarks** are a proprietary, defensible asset: financial claims (Assuraties) are judged only against benchmarks from the same micro-region, inside a fixed seven-bucket P&L taxonomy — Revenue; Operating Expenses; Taxes; Insurance(s); Interest Expense/Income; Miscellaneous Net Expenses/Income; Profit (Net Income) — because taxes, the largest driver of micro-market variability, make cross-region comparison meaningless. Claims that withstand the process are **Assuratized**: reliance-grade, with the evidence trail attached, served through the Reliance API. The HIADR taxonomy and the Content Desk data layer add structured, dual-surface market data on top.\n\n**What is missing.** No purpose-built market reasoning model; no simulated-trading environment in which models or strategies could be trained and tested; no verified strategy marketplace; no machine-payable data product behind the x402 payment tool — the payment rail exists, the data product behind it does not yet. The context-loop flywheel is designed, not evidenced: there is no backtested performance to show, and this document claims none.\n\n### A.3 Fundamental Pillar 3 — Identity, audit trails, and performance-verification infrastructure for non-human financial decision-makers\n\n*When the decision-maker is not human, trust cannot rest on reputation or relationship. It rests on infrastructure: provable identity, a complete audit trail, and verified performance that a counterparty can check without asking permission.*\n\n**What UZE has.** This is the most built-out pillar. Wallet-bound agent identity (§3.2); three-layer gating with enforcement before execution (§3.3); the append-only chronicle under every call (§3.4); the revoke cascade at the call boundary; AssurativeV2's evidence trails, in which a claim's path from Assuraties to Assuratized is itself a verifiable record; and the Talent vertical's demonstration that some gates are human by construction. The pattern across all of it: **an agent's authority is provable, bounded, and revocable, and its history is inspectable.**\n\n**What is missing.** The chronicle is a record, not a proven tamper-proof record — no cryptographic anchoring has been demonstrated. There is no verified performance tracking for agents (no public, checkable record of what an agent's decisions returned), no reputation system, and no \"Know Your Agent\" attestation product. Regulatory literacy is a design stance, not a filed compliance product. Until performance verification exists, Pillar 3 proves *what an agent did* but not yet *how well it did it* — and the second is what counterparties will ultimately price.\n\n### A.4 Supporting pillars\n\nThe three Fundamental Pillars stand on five supporting disciplines, each already stated in §3 and collected here because the surface fails without any one of them:\n\n1. **Wallet-bound identity** — the wallet is the credential; authorization, limits, and history hang off one binding (§3.2).\n2. **Every call a chronicle event** — no silent actions; grants and refusals are recorded with the same care as executions (§3.4).\n3. **Gating before execution** — the fence is checked before the call runs, never audited after the fact as a substitute (§3.3).\n4. **Settlement per rail law** — one cascade for humans and agents alike, with its two carve-outs stated, never improvised (§3.6).\n5. **Discovery through tagging** — listings and GEO/SEO tagging as a designed layer, so agents can find and correctly describe the surface (§3.7).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Addendum B — Two domains, kept separate\n\nThis addendum covers the two financial domains the agentic surface touches. **They are different domains, and they must never be blended** — the same rule the ecosystem applies to its MCP families applies here, one level up:\n\n- **Trading** is included at enterprise and agency level only — an additive, fee-bearing service (TradeLayer · SigLayer), never for individuals and never promoted. It is **not an initiative and not the focus**, and it has **nothing to do with corporate or entity cash flow.**\n- **The Treasury** is about *balances*: the cash-flow and fiat balances an entity holds, and how those balances are worked while they are not needed. It has nothing to do with taking trading positions.\n\nDifferent money, different purpose, different risk, different customer conversation. Part 1 describes the trading inclusion — included, not an initiative, and plainly **not the focus**. Part 2 describes the treasury domain — **the ultimate focus**, and the center of the enterprise-innovation frame in §2.3.\n\n### Part 1 — Trading, included\n\n**What this is.** Trading is an included service at enterprise and agency level only — TradeLayer · SigLayer offered to entity owners, partners, and their sysadmin treasurers; fee-bearing and additive, swaps included, nothing major. It is never offered to individuals, never promoted, and it is not an initiative of this surface. Under the standing mandate, **no agent trades**; SigLayer's discipline is reporting and settlement read from the chain, with discrepancies carried openly as unconfirmed items — an audit posture.\n\n**Doctrine of record (2026-11-20).** The Trading De-Emphasis and Inclusive Rails Doctrine governs: trading is de-emphasized in every diagram and plan; the free and accessible treasury is the promoted focus.\n\n### Part 2 — Digital Asset Treasury — the ultimate focus\n\n**What this domain is.** An entity — a company, a fund, a practice, an operation of any size — holds balances it does not currently need: seasonal cash flow that will be spent in a later quarter, operating reserves above the working line, fiat balances sitting idle between receipt and deployment. Those balances are an asset doing nothing. **Digital Asset Treasury is the discipline of putting them to work — optimizing yield on seasonal or non-required cash-flow and fiat balances — under mandates the entity sets, with records the entity can audit.** It is not trading: no positions are taken against a market view, no risk tolerance is set against price movement. The questions are a treasurer's questions: *how much is truly idle, for how long, at what yield, with what proof, and how fast can it come back?*\n\n**The enterprise offer.** The agentic surface turns that discipline into a service an enterprise can buy:\n\n- **Idle-balance yield optimization as a service.** The entity's balances are assessed for what is genuinely non-required and for how long; allocations are proposed against that assessment; yield is earned on balances that would otherwise sit still. The entity's operating cash is never the subject — only the surplus above it, only for the window it is surplus.\n- **Agents do the watching; the mandate does the deciding.** Agents on this surface monitor balances continuously — inflows, seasonal patterns, upcoming obligations — and *propose* allocations as the picture changes. Execution happens **only inside a gated mandate** the entity set in advance: which balances, what ceiling, what instruments, what return-of-funds terms. The three-layer gating of §3.3 applies unchanged — pre-authorization, enforcement before execution, vertical gates — and **every action is a chronicle event**: proposed, approved by the mandate, executed, settled, reported. The entity can audit any movement after the fact and reconstruct why it happened.\n- **Settlement of record: USDC on Solana**, per the rail law (§3.6) — the default rail, with the cascade behind it. Treasury movements settle on the same law as everything else in the ecosystem; there is no special treasury rail to explain to an auditor.\n- **The treasury home (proposal, unratified).** The 2026-11-07 direction proposes **Altitude** as the treasury home — the level at which SigLayer, TradeLayer, and the test door sit, reserved to the UZE team. It is recorded here as what it is: a **proposal, not a ratified decision**, and the treasury design does not depend on it to be described.\n- **The audit model is SigLayer's reporting discipline.** SigLayer's practice — scheduled reports at fixed times, balances read from the chain rather than asserted, every discrepancy carried openly as an unconfirmed item until evidence resolves it, reports delivered in full — is the standard a treasury client is owed. An enterprise does not hand its idle balances to a system that reports vibes; it hands them to a system whose reporting it has already watched behave correctly when the numbers were uncomfortable.\n\n**Why this is the enterprise-innovation play.** §2.3 framed the surface as optimizing enterprise innovation, and this is where the frame cashes out. An enterprise's product teams innovate on what the enterprise sells; its treasury, in most organizations, innovates on nothing — balances wait, idle, because working them safely used to require attention no one could spare. Agents change that arithmetic: the watching is continuous, the mandate is set once and enforced mechanically, and the record is complete. **Enterprises innovate when their treasury works as hard as their product teams** — when the balance sheet's quiet corners are managed with the same discipline as its loud ones, and the yield on money that was doing nothing funds the next experiment. That is also why this domain, and not trading, is the focus: treasury is a service every enterprise needs, buyable on evidence — reporting quality, gating rigor, audit completeness — that this ecosystem is already building for its own reasons. Fundio's filed direction points the same way: its Enterprise Services level (payroll, payouts, the Command & Control console) already names a self-directed Web3 treasury for business partners as a later extension. The treasury domain is where the agentic surface meets that enterprise offer.\n\n**Honesty box.** No treasury product is live today. No enterprise is being served, no balances are being optimized, no yield is being earned by any client under this design. What exists is the substrate — identity, gating, chronicle, metering, settlement law, and a reporting discipline with a track record — and this design for the service that substrate makes possible. UZD plays no part in it: UZD is a reward only, no ledger for it exists, and treasury settlement is USDC on Solana under the rail law. Anything beyond this paragraph requires James's tap and, in its own time, the licensing and compliance work a treasury service owed to enterprises demands.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What input is being asked of Jonathan\n\nJonathan — this document is being sent to you for input in your lane: the creative interface, and how this system meets people. James is not asking you to supervise the engineering, and nothing here is a build assignment. What would genuinely help:\n\n1. **Legibility of the two domains.** Addendum B insists trading and treasury never be blended. Is that separation as clear on the page as it needs to be — and when this reaches enterprise audiences, what presentation makes the distinction impossible to miss?\n2. **The human side of the pairing.** §2.2 describes the dual-surface contract: console as a view over the API, human-first for judgment. From the creative-interface seat, what does a principal's console need to *feel* like for the chronicle and the gates to read as reassurance rather than machinery?\n3. **Discovery as a creative surface.** §3.7 treats tagging, listings, and feed content as part of the design surface — how agents and the people who direct them find this ecosystem. Your read on how that discovery layer should present, in the channels where this content will actually be tested (Vimeo, YouTube, Instagram), is directly in your Creative Intelligence remit.\n4. **A gut reaction.** Where does this document over-explain, under-explain, or describe something you cannot picture? That reaction, unpolished, is the input.\n\nInput lands the way advisory input always lands in this ecosystem: as proposed amendments, considered by James — not as edits made directly to the system.\n\n---\n\n*Filed 2026-11-19. Companion document: Human Design Surface for UZE Ecosystem (2026-11-19). Sources: the agents-first principle and extensions (James, 2026-11-18); the AGENTIC ECOSYSTEM DESIGN naming and Fundamental Pillars (James, 2026-11-19); the Coinbase Ventures \"Beyond the bot\" mapping (explored 2026-11-19 — company names in its market map were redacted in the source and are named nowhere here); Scope 3 — UZE Instant MCP scope, spec, and descriptive language (filed 2026-11-18); the rail law (2026-11-18); the settlement matrix and Altitude structure (2026-11-17/18); the \"confidential by default\" direction (2026-11-07, proposals labelled unratified).*\n";
